{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-password-compare","kind":"seeded","sample":0,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"Use of standard equality for password hash comparison introduces timing attack vulnerability","category":"security","severity":"high","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/security.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":146,"end_line":146},"explanation":"Comparing passwords or password hashes using standard string equality (==) leaks timing information, allowing an attacker to determine the hash character by character and potentially bypass authentication. Constant-time comparison using hmac.compare_digest should always be used for secrets.","suggested_fix":"Restore hmac.compare_digest(_hash_internal(method, salt, password)[0], hashval)","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[]}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"timing","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":1,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":787,"output_tokens":217,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":false,"requests":1,"cached_requests":0,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":1.551,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-proxy-fix-client-ip","kind":"seeded","sample":0,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"IPv6 host parsing without brackets breaks on ports","category":"correctness","severity":"high","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/middleware/proxy_fix.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":164,"end_line":165},"explanation":"Removing the check for `x_host.endswith(\"]\")` means that an IPv6 address without a port (e.g., `[2001:db8::1]`) will not match `\"partials\"` correctly, but an IPv6 address *with* a port (e.g., `[2001:db8::1]:8080`) will be split incorrectly by `rsplit(\":\", 1)` on the last colon inside the IPv6 address itself rather than the port separator.","suggested_fix":"Restore the check ensuring it is either a plain hostname/IPv4 with a port or an IPv6 address ending with ']' before splitting.","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[]}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"ipv6-host","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":2,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":1038,"output_tokens":302,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":false,"requests":1,"cached_requests":0,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":1.598,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-safe-join-dotdot","kind":"seeded","sample":0,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"Removal of explicit traversal checks allows directory traversal vulnerability","category":"security","severity":"critical","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/security.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":201,"end_line":208},"explanation":"posixpath.normpath does not prevent paths from starting with `..` when the normalized path remains relative (e.g., normpath(\"../foo\") returns \"../foo\"). Removing the checks for `..` and `../` allows untrusted paths to escape the target directory.","suggested_fix":"Restore the checks for `part == \"..\"` and `part.startswith(\"../\")`.","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[]}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"traversal","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":1,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":911,"output_tokens":241,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":false,"requests":1,"cached_requests":0,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":1.401,"error":null}
{"case_id":"seeded-werkzeug-uuid-converter","kind":"seeded","sample":0,"findings":[{"id":"F1","title":"Overly permissive regex allows invalid UUID string formats","category":"correctness","severity":"medium","confidence":"high","anchor":{"path":"src/werkzeug/routing/converters.py","side":"RIGHT","start_line":245,"end_line":245},"explanation":"The regex `[A-Fa-f0-9-]{32,36}` matches strings consisting of 32 to 36 hex digits and hyphens in any position or combination, which accepts malformed strings like `------------------------------------` or misplaced dashes that are not valid UUIDs. While `uuid.UUID(value)` will subsequently raise an exception for truly invalid UUIDs, route matching will capture these malformed strings and cause 500 server errors or unexpected routing behavior instead of a 404 Not Found.","suggested_fix":"Use a regex that explicitly matches either the 32-character hex string or the standard hyphenated format, such as `r'[A-Fa-f0-9]{32}|[A-Fa-f0-9]{8}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{4}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{4}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{4}-[A-Fa-f0-9]{12}'`.","anchor_in_diff":true,"evidence":[]}],"matches":[{"finding_id":"F1","issue_id":"uncaught-value-error","distance":0,"verdict":"match","rationale":"location-only matching"}],"expected_total":1,"decoy_hits":[],"review_usage":{"input_tokens":886,"output_tokens":364,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"judge_usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"cached_input_tokens":0},"cached":false,"requests":1,"cached_requests":0,"agent":null,"duration_seconds":1.894,"error":null}
